---
import InsetText from "@components/inset-text/inset-text.astro";
import Tag from "@components/tag/tag.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/DecisionsRecordLayout.astro";
import Button from "@components/button/button.astro";
---

<Layout>
    <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">002 - Buttons</h1>
    <p class="govuk-body">Date proposed: <strong>24 August 2026</strong></p>
    <Tag colour="blue">Proposal</Tag>

    <h2 class="govuk-heading-l govuk-!-margin-top-8">Context</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        As mentioned in the <a
            href="/decisions-record/adapt-govuk-design-system"
            class="govuk-link">001 - Adapt the GOV.UK Design System</a
        > decision record, the GOV.UK Design System was not designed for web apps
        and as a result inputs, including buttons, have been designed to draw the
        attention of the reader and not be msised when scanning a form to be filled
        in.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The advice on buttons from GOV.UK Design System is to:
    </p>

    <InsetText>
        <i
            >Before adding lots of secondary buttons, try to simplify the page
            or break the content down across multiple pages.
        </i>
    </InsetText>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        However, in the context of web apps which are designed to be used
        multiple times, splitting content to different pages often leads to a
        less user-friendly experience.
    </p>
    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">
        Scenarios where the GOV.UK buttons might need to be adapted:
    </h3>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--number govuk-list--spaced">
        <li>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                <strong>Scenario 1:</strong>
                When buttons are not the primary action of the page, the attention
                of the user is drawn to the buttons and they are more likely to be
                clicked or confused at what they are meant to do.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                In the example below, you can see the <strong>Rename</strong> and
                <strong>Delete</strong> strongly draw the eye whereas the primary
                action is actually to click on the title and view the item in full.
            </p>
            <img
                src="/images/non-primary-action-buttons.webp"
                alt="Screenshot an item with link and a rename and delete button."
                class="govuk-image"
            />
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                <strong>Scenario 2:</strong>
                When there are multiple buttons on the page, the buttons are difficult
                to distinguish from each other and quickly navigate between actions.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                Again the primary action of this page is to read the text
                content below, your attention to the buttons should only be
                drawn when needed. E.g. User first decides they want to download
                the summary, they then look for the download button. As opposed
                to reading all options first and remembering which one they
                wanted to click.
            </p>
            <img
                src="/images/multiple-secondary-buttons.webp"
                alt="Screenshot a page with multiple buttons."
                class="govuk-image"
            />
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                <strong>Scenario 3:</strong>
                Where there are tags and buttons on the same page, it can be difficult
                to distinguish between the two and quickly navigate between actions.
            </p>
            <img
                src="/images/gov-notify-button-and-status-tag-clash.webp"
                alt="Screenshot a page with tags and buttons."
                class="govuk-image"
            />
        </li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Accessibility</h3>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        To be fully accessible, <a
            href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/designing/#ensure-that-interactive-elements-are-easy-to-identify"
            class="govuk-link"
            >interactive elements should be easy to identify</a
        >. Both the similarity to the tag component and the squareness of the
        buttons does not help identify as interactive elements. This was also
        found in <a
            href="https://service-manual.nhs.uk/design-system/components/buttons#research"
            class="govuk-link">research done by the NHS</a
        > where they said that:
    </p>
    <InsetText>
        <p class="govuk-body">
            <i>
                "We based our buttons on the GOV.UK designs. But because our
                logo is a blue rectangle and a number of our components
                (including panel headings) have squared edges, we decided that
                GOV.UK buttons did not stand out enough. The square buttons did
                not look clickable next to the other square components.
            </i>
        </p>
        <p class="govuk-body">
            <i>
                We made the buttons more "buttony" by rounding the corners
                (adding corner radiuses). There is research to suggest that
                rounded corners make things more clickable.
            </i>
        </p>
        <p class="govuk-body">
            <i>
                Our testing confirmed this. Users were able to complete tasks
                using buttons and they did not confuse them with non-clickable
                components."
            </i>
        </p>
    </InsetText>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        Any proposed changes to the buttons should be obviously clickable and
        distinguishable from other components.
    </p>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Decision</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The proposal is to make the following changes to the buttons:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--number govuk-list--spaced">
        <li>
            <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Rounded corners</h3>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                The proposal is to add a border radius to the buttons. This will
                imply greater interactivity than the square corners of the
                GOV.UK buttons.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Primary button colours</h3>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                The suggestion is that introducing the green in the primary
                button in the GOV.UK Design System is deliberately outside of
                the colour palette in order to draw maximum attention to the
                primary action on the page.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                However, in web apps, there are often actions which the
                secondary button is too "quiet" but the green is too "loud".
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                Where this scenario has come up in other GOV.UK services you can
                see they have adopted the Gov UK blue colour as a background:
            </p>
            <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet govuk-list--spaced">
                <li>
                    <h4 class="govuk-heading-s">GOV.UK Notify</h4>
                    <p class="govuk-body">
                        The three main options for API integration have gov
                        around this by using inverted links on a blue
                        background.
                    </p>
                    <img
                        src="/images/govuk-notify-blue-buttons.webp"
                        alt="Screenshot of a gov notify page with three options blue background and inverted links."
                        class="govuk-image"
                    />
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h4 class="govuk-heading-s">Civil service jobs</h4>
                    <p class="govuk-body">
                        The "email me jobs like these" button has a blue
                        background.
                    </p>
                    <img
                        src="/images/civil-service-jobs-blue-button.webp"
                        alt="Screenshot of a civil service jobs page with a green button and a blue button."
                        class="govuk-image"
                    />
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                Rather than have a primary, secondary and tertiary button, the
                proposal is to change the background of the primary button to
                the GOV.UK blue colour <strong>#1d70b8</strong>. This will still
                be within the blue pallete of the overall site but still "loud"
                enough to give the attention of a primary action.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Secondary button colours</h3>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                The background colour of the secondary button is <strong
                    >##f3f3f3</strong
                >. This only has a contrast of <strong>1.1:1</strong> which is very
                light against the white background of the page. This was ok in traditional
                GOV.UK pages as they were designed to be positioned below a form or
                text, normally alongside a primary button so that interactivity was
                clear.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                However, in web apps, there is often the need for buttons to be
                positioned elsewhere on the page, in order to manage users
                attention. In the case of a toolbar, you may have a list of
                multiple buttons, none of which are a primary action. We
                therefore cannot rely on being next to the primary button to
                give the user a clear indication of interactivity.
            </p>
            <p class="govuk-body">
                The proposal is to change the background of the secondary button
                to the GOV.UK light blue colour <strong>#d2e2f1</strong>. This
                is a stronger constrast ratio of <strong>1.32:1</strong> which still
                requires the box shadow for the contrast, but is more clearly interactive
                than the current light grey.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Final proposed design</h3>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        <a href="/components/button" class="govuk-link"
            >Go to button in the component library</a
        >
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The final proposed design has rounded corners and updated colours for
        the primary and secondary buttons.
    </p>
    <div class="component-container govuk-!-margin-bottom-6">
        <Button>Primary button</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary">Secondary button</Button>
    </div>

    <h2 class="govuk-heading-l">Consequences</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        By updating the buttons, there is still the need to differentiate the
        tag component further. This will be addressed in a separate decision
        record.
    </p>
</Layout>
